          <strong>Expectation:</strong> “This stranger probably doesn't want to talk.”
        </p>

        <p style="margin-bottom:10px;">
          <strong>Research finding:</strong> People who engaged strangers often reported a more positive experience than they expected.
        </p>

        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;opacity:0.8;margin-bottom:0;">
          Source: University of Chicago News, research discussed by behavioral scientist Nicholas Epley.
        </p>

      </div>

      <p>In essence, <strong>social confidence grows through exposure</strong>. The more you interact, the less anxious you become about rejection or awkwardness.</p>

      <h3>Why Social Connection Matters</h3>

      <p>Talking to strangers should not be viewed as a replacement for close friendships, family relationships, or professional support. However, brief interactions can be one small way of increasing everyday social contact.</p>

