      <h3>3.2 Start Small</h3>
      <p>If you’re shy, start with manageable interactions: greet a barista, ask for directions, or compliment someone’s outfit. Online, you can practice the same skill by starting with a simple greeting and an open-ended question. Confidence develops gradually through repeated practice.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Practice in an Environment With Clear Safety Controls</h3>

      <p>Online conversation can be useful for communication practice, but anonymity does not eliminate risk. A stranger-chat platform should provide clear ways to leave a conversation, report inappropriate behavior, and block or avoid unwanted interactions where those features are available.</p>

      <p>Technology such as automated moderation can provide an additional layer of protection, but it should never be treated as a guarantee that inappropriate behavior will not occur.</p>

      <p>Before sharing personal information, consider whether the other person actually needs to know it. Avoid sharing passwords, verification codes, financial information, your home address, or other sensitive details with strangers.</p>

      <h2>4. The Psychology Behind Confidence Through Communication</h2>
      <p>Confidence isn’t about never feeling nervous — it’s about acting despite the nerves. Psychologists describe confidence as a byproduct of “exposure therapy” — small, repeated experiences that teach your brain that fear is manageable.</p>

      <h3>4.1 The Confidence Practice Loop</h3>

      <p>A useful way to think about social confidence is as a cycle rather than a single event.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="width:100%;max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;margin:24px 0;">
