      <h2>9. Confidence Can Influence the Tone of a Conversation</h2>

      <p>The way you communicate can influence how an interaction develops. Speaking clearly, listening attentively, showing curiosity, and respecting boundaries can make conversations easier for both people.</p>

      <h3>9.1 Building a Confident Mindset</h3>

      <p>Confidence does not mean assuming every interaction will succeed. A more useful mindset is believing that you can handle different outcomes: a great conversation, an awkward conversation, a short conversation, or no conversation at all.</p>

      <h3>9.2 Helping Others Feel Comfortable</h3>

      <p>Good conversation is reciprocal. Asking thoughtful questions, giving the other person room to speak, and respecting their decision to continue or leave can make an interaction more comfortable for both sides.</p>

      <h2>10. Final Thoughts: Confidence Grows Through Practice</h2>

      <p>Talking to strangers is not a magic formula for becoming confident. What it can provide is something much more practical: repeated opportunities to practice starting conversations, listening, asking questions, handling uncertainty, and recovering from awkward moments.</p>

      <p>Research on brief social interactions suggests that people can sometimes underestimate how positive a conversation with a stranger will be. At the same time, not every interaction will be successful, and becoming socially confident means learning to handle both positive and negative outcomes.</p>

      <p>Online voice and video chat can provide one environment for practicing these skills. The key is to use it intentionally: start with manageable conversations, protect your privacy, respect other people's boundaries, reflect on what you learned, and gradually challenge yourself.</p>

      <p>Confidence is not the absence of nervousness. It is the growing belief that you can participate in a conversation, handle uncertainty, and move forward even when an interaction does not go exactly as planned.</p>

